WEBVTT

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:03.599
Welcome to this Java tutorial, where you'll

00:00:03.599 --> 00:00:06.713
combine values with a reduction and explain why

00:00:06.713 --> 00:00:09.562
an identity and an associative rule support

00:00:09.562 --> 00:00:12.212
correct sequential and parallel results.

00:00:12.867 --> 00:00:16.121
Applications use reductions to turn individual

00:00:16.121 --> 00:00:19.374
contributions into totals and other summaries.

00:00:19.374 --> 00:00:22.699
A valid combining rule also lets an application

00:00:22.699 --> 00:00:25.811
combine partial results without changing the

00:00:25.811 --> 00:00:27.367
meaning of its report.

00:00:28.020 --> 00:00:31.068
Today we'll make a supply report for a campus

00:00:31.068 --> 00:00:31.474
clerk.

00:00:31.474 --> 00:00:34.386
Three deliveries contain two, four, and one

00:00:34.386 --> 00:00:34.725
item.

00:00:34.725 --> 00:00:37.908
Every quantity counts once, and the report must

00:00:37.908 --> 00:00:40.955
total the items using separate sequential and

00:00:40.955 --> 00:00:42.445
parallel computations.

00:00:43.090 --> 00:00:45.356
Both results should be seven items.

00:00:45.356 --> 00:00:47.946
We'll connect that agreement to zero and

00:00:47.946 --> 00:00:50.860
addition, then change the delivery quantities

00:00:50.860 --> 00:00:52.609
and predict another report.

00:00:52.609 --> 00:00:55.587
The comparison checks the results; it does not

00:00:55.587 --> 00:00:57.465
measure which mode is faster.

00:00:58.153 --> 00:01:01.339
Now that we're in the Workspace, let's open

00:01:01.339 --> 00:01:04.228
Main.java and set up our supply report.

00:01:08.114 --> 00:01:10.738
We'll use ArrayList to hold the delivery

00:01:10.738 --> 00:01:11.460
quantities.

00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:14.281
The program's statements go inside the main

00:01:14.281 --> 00:01:17.430
method of our Main class, where this application

00:01:17.430 --> 00:01:17.889
starts.

00:01:26.020 --> 00:01:29.028
Let's record the three deliveries in counts.

00:01:29.028 --> 00:01:31.762
Each Integer entry measures items in one

00:01:31.762 --> 00:01:34.906
delivery, so the total must include each entry

00:01:34.906 --> 00:01:35.795
exactly once.

00:01:47.599 --> 00:01:50.038
Counts now holds two, four, and one.

00:01:50.038 --> 00:01:52.478
We need one total from those values.

00:01:52.478 --> 00:01:54.510
That's the job of a reduction.

00:01:54.510 --> 00:01:57.763
Let's request a fresh sequential stream and give

00:01:57.763 --> 00:01:59.999
reduce zero and an addition rule.

00:02:11.014 --> 00:02:13.849
The two lambda parameters receive values to

00:02:13.849 --> 00:02:16.881
combine, and the expression returns their sum.

00:02:16.881 --> 00:02:19.914
This is the BinaryOperator pattern: two inputs

00:02:19.914 --> 00:02:21.891
and a result of the same type.

00:02:21.891 --> 00:02:25.056
One sequential trace starts at zero, adds two to

00:02:25.056 --> 00:02:28.088
get two, adds four to get six, and adds one to

00:02:28.088 --> 00:02:28.747
get seven.

00:02:28.747 --> 00:02:31.846
Reduce returns that total before the assignment

00:02:31.846 --> 00:02:32.439
finishes.

00:02:32.483 --> 00:02:35.605
Zero is an identity because adding it leaves a

00:02:35.605 --> 00:02:36.691
value unchanged.

00:02:36.691 --> 00:02:39.610
It contributes no extra items and gives the

00:02:39.610 --> 00:02:41.850
result when there are no entries.

00:02:41.850 --> 00:02:44.836
Starting at one would invent an item that no

00:02:44.836 --> 00:02:46.058
delivery supplied.

00:02:46.074 --> 00:02:49.316
Now let's calculate the same report from a fresh

00:02:49.316 --> 00:02:50.397
parallel stream.

00:02:50.397 --> 00:02:53.436
We'll keep both the identity and the addition

00:02:53.436 --> 00:02:54.449
rule unchanged.

00:03:06.147 --> 00:03:08.915
Parallel processing may divide the work and

00:03:08.915 --> 00:03:10.459
combine partial results.

00:03:10.459 --> 00:03:13.420
One possible grouping could total two and four

00:03:13.420 --> 00:03:15.930
to get six, then combine that with one.

00:03:15.930 --> 00:03:18.569
Another could combine four and one first.

00:03:18.569 --> 00:03:20.629
Addition gives seven either way.

00:03:20.629 --> 00:03:23.718
Preserving the result under regrouping is called

00:03:23.718 --> 00:03:24.619
associativity.

00:03:24.619 --> 00:03:27.644
This explanation describes valid groupings, not

00:03:27.644 --> 00:03:29.897
the schedule Java necessarily used.

00:03:29.915 --> 00:03:32.736
The rule also stays independent of changing

00:03:32.736 --> 00:03:33.588
shared state.

00:03:33.588 --> 00:03:36.409
It returns the sum of its inputs instead of

00:03:36.409 --> 00:03:38.573
updating a separate shared total.

00:03:38.573 --> 00:03:41.722
And it leaves counts unchanged while processing,

00:03:41.722 --> 00:03:43.821
which satisfies noninterference.

00:03:43.821 --> 00:03:46.904
Each computation creates a fresh stream because

00:03:46.904 --> 00:03:49.790
its terminal reduction consumes that stream.

00:03:49.867 --> 00:03:53.102
Let's print the two completed totals with clear

00:03:53.102 --> 00:03:55.167
labels and finish the program.

00:04:14.513 --> 00:04:15.918
The report is ready.

00:04:15.918 --> 00:04:19.220
Let's click Run and compare the returned totals

00:04:19.220 --> 00:04:21.538
with the seven items we expected.

00:04:27.112 --> 00:04:28.631
Both lines report seven.

00:04:28.631 --> 00:04:31.606
Each calculation includes all three deliveries,

00:04:31.606 --> 00:04:34.074
and neither adds an extra contribution.

00:04:34.074 --> 00:04:36.986
The caller prints Sequential first because the

00:04:36.986 --> 00:04:39.771
print statements run in their written order.

00:04:39.771 --> 00:04:42.809
These lines show complete results, not the order

00:04:42.809 --> 00:04:45.531
in which parallel work processed individual

00:04:45.531 --> 00:04:46.037
entries.

00:04:46.079 --> 00:04:49.095
Let's change only the delivery quantities to

00:04:49.095 --> 00:04:50.467
five, zero, and six.

00:04:50.467 --> 00:04:53.620
Keeping the two computations unchanged lets us

00:04:53.620 --> 00:04:56.842
check their rules with another input, including

00:04:56.842 --> 00:04:59.379
a delivery that contributes no items.

00:05:07.568 --> 00:05:10.232
Before running, predict both output lines.

00:05:10.232 --> 00:05:13.277
Explain the difference between the zero entry in

00:05:13.277 --> 00:05:16.323
counts and the zero identity supplied to reduce.

00:05:16.323 --> 00:05:18.797
Then give two possible groupings of the

00:05:18.797 --> 00:05:21.715
quantities that preserve their order and check

00:05:21.715 --> 00:05:23.618
whether those groupings agree.

00:05:31.669 --> 00:05:35.002
Let's click Run and compare your prediction with

00:05:35.002 --> 00:05:36.044
the new report.

00:05:41.265 --> 00:05:42.816
Both totals are eleven.

00:05:42.816 --> 00:05:46.054
The zero list entry describes a delivery with no

00:05:46.054 --> 00:05:49.156
items, while the identity is the neutral value

00:05:49.156 --> 00:05:50.640
used by the reduction.

00:05:50.640 --> 00:05:53.338
Neither changes the sum of five and six.

00:05:53.338 --> 00:05:56.440
Grouping five with zero first or zero with six

00:05:56.440 --> 00:05:58.126
first still gives eleven.

00:05:58.126 --> 00:06:01.364
The inputs changed, but the valid combining rule

00:06:01.364 --> 00:06:02.915
did not need to change.

00:06:02.965 --> 00:06:05.801
You used reduction to make one result, checked

00:06:05.801 --> 00:06:08.020
the neutral identity and associative

00:06:08.020 --> 00:06:10.917
combination, and kept the source and processing

00:06:10.917 --> 00:06:13.568
rules independent of changing shared state.

00:06:13.568 --> 00:06:16.342
Equal totals support this result, but they do

00:06:16.342 --> 00:06:18.438
not establish a speed improvement.

00:06:18.438 --> 00:06:21.336
For your next design, choose a numerical report

00:06:21.336 --> 00:06:24.172
and explain its contributions, combining rule,

00:06:24.172 --> 00:06:27.007
and empty-input result before deciding whether

00:06:27.007 --> 00:06:29.165
parallel processing is appropriate.
